### Backend entry (`PackRegistration`)

Your `__generated__/pack-entry.ts` exports a registration object (`@apackdev/sdk/framework`):

```typescript
export const registration: PackRegistration = {
  id: string;
  features?: Record<string, PackFeature>;  // by feature id: { designation?, system?: { machine, receives, early? }, surface?: { receives }, services?, settings? }
  services?: Record<string, unknown>;
  ears?: PackEARS;                 // entities, relKinds
  lifecycle?: PackLifecycleHooks;            // onInit/onShutdown (lifecycle.onInit / lifecycle.onShutdown)
  migrations?: PackMigration[];    // { target, description, up }
  repositories?: Record<string, unknown>;  // features.<id>.repositories, registered with the app's engine
  steps?: StepDefinition[];
  artifacts?: ArtifactDefinition[];
  blocks?: BlockDefinition[];
  content.writers?: Record<string, ContentWriter>;  // apack.json `content.writers`, keyed by entity type
  appliers?: ContentApplier[];              // one per content.sources key, which applying the pack's compiled content runs
};
```

The app runs each feature at `<packId>/<featureId>`. Its declared contract supplies system inputs and outputs and surface inboxes; the build writes those event names into static snapshot data and generates matching runtime bindings. Explicit `system.events.incoming` entries add routing names. Shared SDK lifecycle events remain implicit. Discovery reads the catalog without activating the pack; the bus uses the captured contracts of endpoints that actually bound. A machine's private handlers do not add public inbox entries.

The runtime bundle also exports `setCompiledDir(dir)`, which the host calls with the directory of the pack's compiled content.

The native host frontend registers the `reference` document node from `@apackdev/ui` before external
pack frontends load. Its declaration is part of the common native catalog. Parsing saved references
therefore does not require default-setup; that pack owns only its suggestion extension and popup UI.

### Frontend entry (`PackFERegistration`)

Your generated frontend entry exports its declaration revision and executable bindings:

```typescript
export default {
  id: string;
  declarationRevision: string;
  features?: Record<string, {
    surface?: SurfaceDefinition;
    designation?: string;
    default?: true;
    settingsForm?: Component;
    references?: ReferenceBindings;
  }>;
  settingsForms?: Record<string, Component>;
  steps?: StepDefinition[];
  textEditor?: Record<string, TextEditorExtensions>;
  appExtensions?: Record<string, Component>;
  artifacts?: ArtifactDefinition[];
  blocks?: BlockDefinition[];
} satisfies PackFERegistration;
```

Both entry files are generated by `apack pack build`. The host admits installed frontend bindings against
its accepted backend declarations and revision. Native host composition supplies its own declarative
contract. Serving a frontend through a development server changes delivery, not declaration authority.
Pack code contributes through its registration; it cannot write the host's registry directly.

Frontend preparation captures metadata and slot membership before checking or binding settings. It keeps
actual machines, components and callbacks intact. Refused or stale candidates preserve accepted lookups;
provisional settings resources are retired. Catalog declarations have a separate lifetime from active code.
`getStepFrontendBindings()` from `@apackdev/sdk/fe` exposes active step bindings with the accepted owner,
declaration and host-issued revisions. Runtime/build revisions identify the accepted backend; source
delivery is a separate concern. Native bindings have no installed build stamp.

Native content declares `content.writers` and `content.sources.<key> = { applier: "path.ts" }`.
Writers must belong to entities declared by that native pack, under a feature or `data.entities`.
These references describe composition bindings; discovery never imports them. Native appliers run
through `importCompiledContent` with an explicitly supplied compiled-content directory. Native
composition does not declare source paths/formats or datasets to compile, and automatic application
continues to target installed builds. Both native and built backend bindings must match declared writer
and applier names before receiving runtime authority.

Native composition may also declare the same `migrations.app` and `migrations.pack` maps as authored
packs. App-line changes use the app version. Pack-line changes require the native owner's `version`;
it is never inferred from the app version. Checked migration targets, descriptions and callbacks are
captured at admission. Native and installed owners share migration targeting and dependency ordering,
while discovery reads only declaration data. No installed directory or manifest is invented for native
migrations.

### Structure

```typescript
import type { ActionMeta } from '@apackdev/sdk/build';
import type { Services, Z } from '#generated/services.ts';

export const meta: ActionMeta = {
  label: 'Analyze Text',
  description: 'Summarizes text and classifies its intent',
  category: 'analysis',
  input: {
    text: {
      type: 'string',
      description: 'The text to analyze',
      required: true,
      placeholder: 'e.g. I need help setting up my account',
    },
  },
};

export async function action(
  params: Record<string, any>,
  services: Services,
  z: Z,
  flowId: string,
) {
  const { text } = params;

  // Use a prompt template
  const summaryPrompt = services.prompt.usePrompt('Summarize Text', {
    text,
    maxSentences: 2,
  });

  // Generate text with a model (provider:model id)
  const result = await services.inference.generateText({
    model: 'anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-5',
    instructions: 'Answer in two sentences.',
    prompt: summaryPrompt || text,
  });

  // Generate structured output with the injected zod
  const { output: classified } = await services.inference.generateText({
    model: 'openai:gpt-5-mini',
    prompt: `Classify the intent of this text: ${text}`,
    output: { type: 'object', schema: z.object({ intent: z.string(), confidence: z.number() }) },
  });

  // Log results
  services.logger.info('Analysis complete', { summary: result.text });

  return {
    summary: result.text,
    intent: classified.intent,
  };
}
```

### Structure

```typescript
import type { PromptMeta } from '@apackdev/sdk/build';

export const meta: PromptMeta = {
  label: 'Summarize Text',
  description: 'Generates a concise summary of the provided text',
  category: 'analysis',
  inputs: {
    text: {
      name: 'text',
      type: 'string',
      required: true,
      description: 'The text to summarize',
    },
    maxSentences: {
      name: 'maxSentences',
      type: 'number',
      defaultValue: 3,
      description: 'Maximum sentences in summary',
    },
  },
  outputSchema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      summary: { type: 'string', description: 'The generated summary' },
    },
  },
};

export function template(params: Record<string, any>) {
  const maxSentences = params.maxSentences || 3;

  return `Summarize the following text in ${maxSentences} sentence(s) or fewer.

Text:
${params.text}`;
}
```

### Structure

```typescript
import type { FlowDSL } from '@apackdev/sdk/build';
import { entry, on, keepAlive, action, fire, branch, subflow } from '#generated/flow-helpers.ts';

export default {
  // Linear: entry -> action -> fire
  'Analysis Flow': [
    entry([
      action('Analyze Text', { label: 'analyze' }),
      fire('analysis.complete', { label: 'notify' }),
    ]),
  ],

  // Long-running: init + keep alive + event listeners
  'Monitor Flow': [
    entry([
      action('Initialize', { label: 'init' }),
      keepAlive(),
    ]),
    on('user.message', [[
      action('Process', { label: 'process' }),
      fire('message.processed'),
    ]], 'Handle Message'),
  ],

  // Branching: action -> conditional branches
  'Support Flow': [
    entry([
      action('Classify', { label: 'classify' }),
      branch(
        [
          // A bare key reads the previous step's result: Classify returned { intent }
          { if: "intent == 'question'", steps: [
            action('Lookup', { label: 'lookup', map: { question: '$.event.data.payload.text' } }),
          ]},
          { if: "intent == 'request'", steps: [
            action('Process Request', { label: 'process' }),
          ]},
        ],
        [fire('support.escalated', { label: 'escalate' })],
      ),
    ]),
  ],

  // Parallel entry branches
  'Setup Flow': [
    entry(
      [action('Load Config', { label: 'config' })],
      [action('Warm Cache', { label: 'cache' })],
      [keepAlive()],
    ),
  ],

  // Sub-flow delegation
  'Orchestrator Flow': [
    entry([
      subflow('Analysis Flow', { label: 'run analysis' }),
      fire('orchestration.complete'),
    ]),
  ],
} satisfies FlowDSL;
```

## Manifest configuration

Point your manifest at the content directories:

```json
{
  "content": {
    "sources": {
      "actions": "src/content/actions",
      "prompts": "src/content/prompts",
      "flows": "src/content/flows",
      "documents": "src/content/documents",
      "commands": "src/content/commands"
    }
  }
}
```

A specialty key takes its path as a string or `{ "path": … }`; any other key is a [content source](#writing-entities). `apack pack build` compiles each key into `<key>.content.json` (media into `media/<key>/`) and writes `content.json`, which names the pack and indexes the keys and their items for Settings → Import Pack Content. Writing runs at boot and when a pack is installed or reloaded, in `replace-on-collision` mode, and is skipped when the compiled output's hash hasn't changed. The hash covers every written key's compiled file, so changing one key's source re-runs the pack's boot apply, its other rows still being skipped by their own hashes. At boot, packs content in dependency order, so a pack's content can reference what a pack it declares a dependency on written. An apply that reports errors fails: an external pack's error is recorded on its installed-packs entry, and the same output isn't retried until it changes — or until one of the packs it depends on content, since that is the other thing that can change the outcome. Installing the pack again is also a fresh attempt, even at the version already installed: an install replaces the compiled files, and what was last written is remembered as the files and not only their contents.

## Writing entities

Content rows of an entity type from markdown or JSON in two parts of `apack.json`:

- **A format** in `content.formats` says how a source becomes records: a built-in format (`markdown-tree` or `json`) or a compiler module, and the settings it uses.
- **A content source** in `content.sources` names a source and the format that compiles it: `{ "path", "format" }`. The entry key is yours to name.

```json
{
  "features": {
    "memos": {
      "description": "Short notes the pack ships.",
      "entities": { "Memo": { "shape": "src/features/memos/be/types.ts#MemoAttributes" } }
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "formats": {
      "memos": {
        "format": "markdown-tree",
        "entity": "Memo",
        "identity": ["title", "parent"],
        "tree": { "branch": "index.md", "relKind": "contains" },
        "fields": {
          "title": { "from": "frontmatter.title", "default": "filename", "type": "string" },
          "pinned": { "from": "frontmatter.pinned" },
          "text": { "from": "body" }
        },
        "media": "media"
      }
    },
    "sources": {
      "memos": { "path": "src/content/memos", "format": "memos" }
    }
  }
}
```

An entry can't set or change any format settings; a pack that needs different settings defines its own format. Format names are lowercase with hyphens.

| Format field | Description |
|---|---|
| `format` / `compiler` | Exactly one: `markdown-tree`, `json`, or a compiler module path (see [Compiler modules](#compiler-modules)) |
| `entity` | The entity type the items are written as, or an array of types. Each record's `entity` must be one of them (and `tree.branchEntity`); a built-in format tags records with `entity` only when it's a single string. Omitted, the source is a dataset: compiled and never written, so it belongs under `content.datasets`. Each type must be declared by the pack, a dependency or the SDK |
| `identity` | Fields matched to find an existing row. `"parent"` also requires the row to be linked from the record's tree parent by `tree.relKind`. Required unless the entity type has a `find` hook: writing a record without either fails |
| `tree.branch` | A directory's own file (`index.md`) giving the directory's frontmatter and body |
| `tree.branchEntity` | The entity type directories become; defaults to `entity` |
| `tree.relKind` | The relation from a parent row to each child row; defaults to `contains` |
| `fields` | `markdown-tree` only (the manifest rejects it elsewhere): record field → `{ from, default?, type? }` |
| `media` | A relative directory under an entry's `path` (no `.` or `..` segments), copied with the content |

### JSON

```json
"content": {
  "formats": { "tags": { "format": "json", "entity": "Tag", "identity": ["name"] } },
  "sources": { "tags": { "path": "src/content/tags.json", "format": "tags" } }
}
```

The file holds an array of records (or `{ "records": [...] }`); a record may carry its own `entity` and `children`.

### Compiler modules

When a source needs parsing that field sources can't express, give the format a `compiler` module instead of a built-in `format`. Its default export gets a `ContentCompileContext` (`key`, `path`: the entry's absolute path, `packDir`: the writing pack's directory, `format`) and returns records, or a promise of them, each tagged with its `entity`:

```typescript
// src/content/compilers/glossary.ts
import { compileMarkdownTree, type ContentCompileContext, type ContentItem } from '@apackdev/sdk/build';

export default function compileGlossary({ path, format }: ContentCompileContext): ContentItem[] {
  // Skip the format's media directory, if it has one
  return compileMarkdownTree(path, { media: format.media }).map((item) => ({
    entity: 'Term',
    term: String(item.frontmatter.term ?? item.displayName),
    definition: item.body.trim(),
  }));
}
```

```json
"content": {
  "formats": { "glossary": { "compiler": "src/content/compilers/glossary.ts", "entity": "Term", "identity": ["term"] } },
  "sources": { "glossary": { "path": "src/content/glossary", "format": "glossary" } }
}
```

The build loads TypeScript compiler modules itself, and bundles every compiler module your formats name into `dist/build/content-compilers.mjs` so packs depending on yours can use those formats. A record's `contentHash` defaults to a hash of its fields (and its children's hashes); set it yourself to decide what counts as a change. A format without `entity` is compiled but not written: pack code reads `<key>.content.json` (default-setup's FAQs work this way).

### ContentApplier modules

An entry `{ "applier": "src/content/custom.ts" }` (no `path` or `format`) replaces the format and the format applier with the module's named export `apply`, which the generated `appliers.ts` puts in the pack's registration under the content key. The build compiles nothing for it, so the module brings its own data.

An entry with all three, `{ "path", "format", "applier" }`, is compiled with the format, and the module's `content` content the compiled `<key>.content.json` instead of the format applier, even when the format names no entity — for content whose rows the format applier cannot place, where the compiler still does the reading.

```typescript
// src/content/custom.ts
import type { ApplyContext, ApplyResult } from '@apackdev/sdk/utils';

export function apply(ctx: ApplyContext): ApplyResult {
  ctx.log('  custom content');
  return { created: 0, updated: 0, skipped: 0 };
}
```

| `ApplyContext` | Description |
|---|---|
| `compiledDir` | The pack's compiled content directory |
| `include?` | This key's include set (see [Include sets](#include-sets)) |
| `mode?` | `'keep-existing' \| 'replace-on-collision' \| 'wipe-and-replace'` (see [Change tracking](#change-tracking)) |
| `log(...args)` | Logs when writing is verbose |

`apply` is synchronous and returns `ApplyResult`: `created`, `updated`, `skipped`, and `errors?: string[]`, where a non-empty list fails the run.

### A dependency's formats

An entry can name a format of a pack it depends on as `"<pack id>:<name>"`. It compiles your sources with that pack's settings and compiler module, so you get the same records it would:

```json
{
  "dependencies": { "default-setup": "*" },
  "content": {
    "sources": {
      "team-notes": { "path": "src/content/notes", "format": "default-setup:notes" }
    }
  }
}
```

default-setup's formats:

| Format | Content | Sources |
|---|---|---|
| `notes` | `Note` | Markdown notes; frontmatter `title`, `type` (`document`, `tasklist`, `task`), `icon`, `favorite`, `hideCompletedChildren`, `completed`. A directory is a parent note, its `index.md` giving the parent's frontmatter and content |
| `faqs` | none (compiled only) | The Help tab's FAQs; not useful to other packs |

Any pack can define a format for any entity type it can write (its own, a dependency's or the SDK's). Only the pack that declares an entity type defines its content writers.

### Content writers

Without hooks, the applier writes rows directly: it matches existing rows on the format's `identity`, creates new ones with their fields, and links children with `tree.relKind`. When an entity type needs more — shortCodes, ordering, validation, derived links — the pack that declares the type registers content writers for it:

```json
{
  "features": {
    "memos": {
      "description": "Short notes the pack ships.",
      "entities": { "Memo": { "shape": "src/features/memos/be/types.ts#MemoAttributes" } }
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "writers": { "Memo": "src/content/writers/memos.ts#memoContentWriter" }
  }
}
```

```typescript
// src/content/writers/memos.ts
import type { ContentWriter, ContentItem } from '@apackdev/sdk/content';
import { repository } from '#generated/repository.ts';

export const memoContentWriter: ContentWriter<ContentItem & { title: string; text: string; pinned?: boolean }> = {
  find: (record) => repository.memoQueries.byTitle(record.title),       // { id, contentHash } | undefined
  create: (record, { parentId, index }) => repository.memoCommands.add({ title: record.title, text: record.text, pinned: record.pinned }).id,
  // clearedFields: fields the last apply set that the item no longer sets, reset to a new memo's
  update: (id, record, { clearedFields }) => repository.memoCommands.update(id, {
    title: record.title,
    text: record.text,
    pinned: record.pinned ?? (clearedFields.includes('pinned') ? false : undefined),
  }),
  remove: (id) => repository.memoCommands.delete(id),
};
```

| `ContentWriter<R>` member | Called | Returns |
|---|---|---|
| `container?: boolean` | Not called: it marks the entity a holder of other records (a folder) | — |
| `find?(record, context)` | To match a record that no row's `contentKey` matches; replaces `identity` | `{ id, contentHash? }` or `undefined` |
| `create?(record, context)` | For a record with no row | The new row's id |
| `update?(id, record, context)` | For a changed record whose row isn't edited | — |
| `remove?(id)` | By `wipe-and-replace`, and to undo a create whose media copy or stamping failed | — |

`container` is how two packs share a folder. An entity another item's apply claimed (another pack's, or another entry's of your pack) is normally not yours, whatever its name, so your item writes an entity of its own beside it. When the entity's hooks set `container: true`, your record's `find` match is reused as the parent instead: its children are written under it in every mode (`keep-existing` skips only the children that exist), and the row itself is never updated, stamped or re-keyed — it stays the item that wrote it. The run counts it as skipped. Whichever item creates the entity first owns it: the fields another item sets on it (a folder's description) are dropped. The entity is found by `find` on every apply, so if the user renames or moves it, the next apply creates the folder again where your records expect it. The SDK sets it on `Collection`, so a pack writing `internal/commands/mine.md` under its `documents` or `commands` key puts its document in the existing folders rather than forking them. Its `find` hooks match a name within `parentId`, so a folder of the same name elsewhere is a different entity.

`ContentWriteContext`: `parentId?` (the tree parent's entity), `index` (the record's position among its siblings), `clearedFields: string[]` (on `update`, the fields the last apply set that the item no longer sets; empty for `find` and `create`). A missing hook falls back to the format applier's behavior. A hook that throws fails that record (reported in the run's errors) and writing moves on.

`create` and `update` store the record's fields under their names, as the record gives them, and `update` writes every field the record sets: change tracking records those fields' stored values. `update` also resets the fields in `clearedFields` to what `create` gives a record that doesn't set them (see [Change tracking](#change-tracking)). Hooks are keyed by entity type, not by format, so every pack that writes `Memo` — with your format, its own, or one depending on yours — goes through them. A `find` hook replaces the format's `identity`. default-setup registers hooks for `Note`. `Document` and `Collection` are the SDK's entities, and so are their hooks — a pack declares hooks only for entity types it owns, so these need no registration and are always present. Either way, entities written with those formats get the same shortCodes, display order and links as rows created in the app.

### Theirs or offer: what happens to an item the user edited

Every outcome above leaves the user's edit alone. What `onUserEdit` decides is whether anyone is **told** — and the line is whether editing the item meant adoption or customisation.

```json
{
  "content": {
    "sources": {
      "prompts": { "path": "src/content/prompts" },
      "actions": { "path": "src/content/actions", "onUserEdit": "offer" }
    }
  }
}
```

- **`theirs`** (the default) — the item is the user's from the first edit, and the app never mentions it again. Where your content is a starting point for their own writing, this is what you want: no badge about their own prose.
- **`offer`** — the item is yours, customised. The app records that a newer version is waiting, and the Packs view puts it to the user with the parts they changed. They still get your bug fixes if they want them.

The default is the conservative one, so the option that interrupts someone is the one you have to type. default-setup declares `offer` for its `actions`, `prompts` and `flows`, and nothing else.

Neither value overwrites what the user wrote, and neither makes a second copy of anything — there is one entity either way. The pair is named for whose the item is, because the only thing that differs is whether your pack goes on offering its version.

**What an offer looks like to the user.** The pack's row in the Packs list carries a count of the items waiting on them; opening it gives *Your changes to this pack's content*, one row per item: what it is, which parts of it they changed, and what the pack's version would do. Two choices for a newer version —

- **Take the new version**, which writes the pack's item over theirs. There is nothing stored to restore from, and the button says so.
- **Keep mine**, which records the version they declined. A later release that leaves this item alone is then silent, and one that changes the item asks again.

— and for an item you have stopped shipping, **keep it** (it becomes plainly theirs, and the row moves to *Items you keep your own version of*) or **delete it**.

An item the user kept their own version of also gets **Reset to factory**, which is the same write as taking an offered version: one item, overwritten, because they asked.

## Steps

A step definition adds a new node type to the visual flow editor. A `StepDefinition` (`@apackdev/sdk/steps`) has these fields:

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `type` | `string` | Node type id (`node.nodeType`) and DSL `type` |
| `node` | `StepNodeFacet` | Inline manifest labels/defaults emitted into both registrations |
| `kind` | `'step' \| 'trigger'` | Omitted means `'step'`. Trigger nodes start tracks and never run as steps |
| `build` | `StepBuildFacet` | Compile/validate/decompile DSL nodes |
| `runtime` | `StepRuntimeFacet` | Execute the step |
| `fe` | `StepFEFacet` | Palette, canvas node and form |
| `trigger` | `TriggerFacet` | For `kind: 'trigger'` |
| `dsl` | `StepDSLMeta` | Same shape as the manifest entry's `dsl`; codegen reads the manifest's (see [Flow helpers](#flow-helpers)) |

Declare initial values once in `apack.json`:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "steps": {
      "my-step": {
        "node": {
          "label": "My Step",
          "defaults": {
            "enabled": true
          }
        },
        "build": {
          "compile": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#compile",
          "validate": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#validate",
          "getLabel": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#getLabel"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`node.defaults` contains JSON data and must satisfy the step's validation. New nodes receive independent
copies, so editing a nested field does not change the next node's baseline. No node-data export is needed.

### Build facet (build.ts)

```typescript
import type { StepBuildFacet, StepCompileResult, StepValidationError } from '@apackdev/sdk/steps';
import { EARS } from '@apackdev/sdk';
import type { DSLMyStepNode } from './types.ts';

export const compile: StepBuildFacet['compile'] = (node, nodeId, ts, ctx): StepCompileResult => {
    const step = node as unknown as DSLMyStepNode;
    return {
        entity: { id: nodeId, entityType: EARS.Entity.Node, createdAt: ts, nodeType: 'my-step', label: step.label ?? 'My Step' },
        relations: [],
    };
};

export const validate: StepBuildFacet['validate'] = (step, path, ctx): StepValidationError[] => {
    return [];
};

export const getLabel: StepBuildFacet['getLabel'] = (step, index) => {
    return typeof step.label === 'string' ? step.label : `My Step ${index}`;
};
```

| Member | Signature | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `compile` | `(node, nodeId, ts, ctx) => { entity, relations }` | DSL node → `Node` entity. `ctx` (`StepCompileContext`) maps `actions`, `prompts` and `flows` names to ids. `relations` are `{ source, kind, target, info? }` |
| `validate` | `(step, path, ctx) => StepValidationError[]` | Each error is `{ path, message }`. `ctx` has `actions`, `prompts`, `flowNames`, `nodeLabels` (sets), `path`, `skipReferenceCheck?` |
| `getLabel` | `(step, index) => string` | Label for a DSL step at `index` |
| `decompile?` | `(node, ctx) => dslNode` | Entity → DSL (reverse of `compile`). `ctx` has `actionMap`, `promptMap`, `flowMap` and `resolveBranch?(sourceNodeId, sourceHandle)`. Omitted → `{ type, label }` |
| `relation?` | `{ field, targetEntity }` | The node field holding an id linked by an `instance_of` relation to a `targetEntity` row (the `action` step: `{ field: 'actionId', targetEntity: 'Action' }`); flow editing and export fill the field from the link |
| `branches?` | `(dslNode) => { key, steps }[]` | Inline step lists nested in the node. The compiler compiles each and wires branch `i` from this node with `sourceHandle: 'branch-<i>'`, continuing to the next step afterwards |

### Types (types.ts)

```typescript
import type { NodeBase } from '@apackdev/sdk';
import type { DSLNodeBase } from '@apackdev/sdk/build';

export interface DSLMyStepNode extends DSLNodeBase {
  type: 'my-step';
  label?: string;
}

export interface MyStepNode extends NodeBase {
  nodeType: 'my-step';
}
```

`generate-entries` re-exports every definition's `types.ts` from `#generated/step-types`, and each `export interface … extends NodeBase` joins the pack's `Node` row union.

### Manifest

A step is declared in `apack.json` under `extensions.steps`, keyed by its type, with each facet named where its code is:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "steps": {
      "my-step": {
        "build": {
          "compile": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#compile",
          "validate": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#validate",
          "getLabel": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#getLabel"
        },
        "fe": {
          "nodeConfig": {
            "icon": "Box",
            "isImplemented": true,
            "category": "logic",
            "connectionRules": {
              "inputs": -1,
              "outputs": -1
            }
          },
          "colorKey": "indigo",
          "components": {
            "form": {
              "entry": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/form.vue",
              "load": "lazy"
            }
          }
        },
        "runtime": {
          "handler": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/runtime.ts#handler",
          "isAsync": true
        },
        "dsl": {
          "primaryField": "action"
        },
        "node": {
          "label": "My Step"
        },
        "types": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/types.ts"
      },
      "my-trigger": {
        "kind": "trigger",
        "trigger": {
          "trackField": "myTrigger",
          "compile": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/build.ts#compile",
          "decompile": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/build.ts#decompile",
          "register": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/runtime.ts#register"
        },
        "fe": {
          "nodeConfig": {
            "icon": "Radio",
            "isImplemented": true,
            "category": "trigger",
            "connectionRules": {
              "inputs": 0,
              "outputs": -1
            }
          },
          "colorKey": "blue",
          "components": {
            "form": {
              "entry": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/form.vue",
              "load": "lazy"
            }
          }
        },
        "node": {
          "label": "My Trigger"
        },
        "types": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/types.ts"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

| Field | What it is |
|---|---|
| `kind` | `step` (the default) or `trigger` |
| `build` | An object declaring compile/validate/getLabel references, optional decompile/branches references and inline relation metadata. A trigger declares `trigger` instead |
| `trigger` | Track metadata (`trackField`, optional `persistent`/`queryFields`) and compile/decompile references, with optional validation/register references |
| `node` | Inline labels and initial JSON node fields |
| `fe` | Inline frontend declaration (above) |
| `types` | Explicit module declaring node and DSL types |
| `runtime` | `{ handler?, isAsync?, waits?, spawnsSubflow? }` |
| `dsl` | DSL configuration for flow-helper generation (below) |

Codegen sends each facet where it is used, so no barrel keeps them in step with each other: `build` and
`runtime` to the pack's backend registration, `fe` to its frontend one, and `build` (or `trigger`) alone to
`src/__generated__/steps-build.ts`, which `apack pack build` bundles to `dist/build/steps.build.mjs` — the
module a *dependent* pack's build loads to validate its flows with your step code. A dependency's step types
are loaded that way too, and a type this pack and a dependency both define fails the build.

Step `types` and `dsl.helpers` explicitly name pack-root-relative modules. No contribution is inferred
from neighboring files or the directory of another facet.

### Manifest

An artifact is declared in `apack.json` under `extensions.artifacts`, keyed by its type:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "artifacts": {
      "chart": { "icon": "BarChart3", "fe": "src/extensions/artifacts/viewers/chart-artifact.vue" }
    }
  }
}
```

| Field | Required | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `icon` | yes | The name of a `lucide-vue-next` export. That is the icon set the host provides every pack's frontend, so a pack names one rather than shipping a component; a name lucide doesn't export fails the pack's typecheck |
| `fe` | no | The viewer that opens it — a `.vue` file, taken by its default export |

Both of an artifact's facets are frontend ones, so the backend registration carries the type alone and the
icon set never reaches a pack's backend bundle.

### Manifest

A block is declared in `apack.json` under `extensions.blocks`, keyed by its type. Each entry names where its code lives, and codegen
splits the two facets between the entries: the component goes into the pack's frontend bundle, the backend
facet into its runtime, so a backend process never loads a Vue component to know a block exists.

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "blocks": {
      "rating": { "fe": "src/extensions/blocks/display/RatingBlock.vue" },
      "color-picker": { "kind": "input", "fe": "src/extensions/blocks/input/ColorPickerInput.vue" }
    }
  }
}
```

| Field | Required | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `kind` | no | `display` (the default) or `input` |
| `fe` | no | The component that draws it — a `.vue` file, taken by its default export. The host renders it |
| `be` | no | `"path#exportName"` of its `BlockBEFacet` |

The key is the type, so a pack cannot declare one twice, and a path naming no file fails the build naming
the block and the path.

### Sending blocks from the backend

default-setup's threads feature provides `services.chat.sendBlockMessage({ threadId, text, blocks, forkable?, autoHide?, asUser?, asideContext? })`: it adds an assistant message with the blocks and sends `MESSAGE_ADDED` to the threads surface, returning `{ messageId }`. `autoHide: true` requires `asUser` (boolean); `asideContext` overrides the label used in the aside text.

```typescript
services.chat.sendBlockMessage({
  threadId,
  text: 'Pick a color',
  blocks: [{ type: 'color-picker', props: { label: 'Accent' } }],
});
```

#### Pairing a prompt with an input

A message's `blocks` array is rendered in order, so the convention for asking a question is two blocks: a `prompt` display block carrying the question text, followed by the input block that collects the answer. The message's own `text` is what shows in thread summaries; the `prompt` block is what the user reads above the control.

```typescript
services.chat.sendBlockMessage({
  threadId,
  text: 'Choose a project directory',
  blocks: [
    { type: 'prompt', props: { content: 'Which directory should I work in?' } },
    { type: 'file-picker', props: { fileType: 'directory', allowMultiple: false, displayText: 'Selected project:' } },
  ],
});
```

The same shape covers the other input types. Each row below is one `blocks` array:

| Asking for | Blocks |
|---|---|
| A file or directory | `prompt`, then `file-picker` with `{ fileType: 'file' \| 'directory' \| 'both', allowMultiple, displayText }` |
| Free text | `prompt`, then `text` with `{ placeholder, multiline, required, displayText, suggestions }` |
| One of several options | `prompt`, then `choice` |
| Confirmation | `prompt`, then `approval` |
| An action to run | `prompt` (optional), then `button-group` with `{ buttons, keepInteractive, displayText }` |

A `link` block needs no input block — it is a display block listing navigation targets, and the leading `prompt` is optional:

```typescript
services.chat.sendBlockMessage({
  threadId,
  text: 'Opening settings',
  blocks: [{ type: 'link', props: { links } }],
});
```

#### Button groups

`button-group` is the one input block the backend drives after the first response, so it has two modes. Both follow the same path: frontend → backend → database → `UPDATE_MESSAGE_STATE` → frontend.

- **`toggleStates`** — an on/off pair. The backend flips `state` between the two itself.
- **`states`** — a map of named states, where a flow decides which one comes next.

Each `ButtonConfig` (`@apackdev/sdk/blocks`) is `{ id, label, state }` plus one of those two maps; every state entry carries its own `label`, optional `variant` and optional `disabled`. Pass `keepInteractive: true` to leave the buttons live after a response instead of disabling them.

```typescript
services.chat.sendBlockMessage({
  threadId,
  text: 'Quick toggles:',
  blocks: [{
    type: 'button-group',
    props: {
      buttons: [
        // Auto-cycling: the backend flips between on and off
        {
          id: 'watch',
          label: 'Watch',
          state: 'off',
          toggleStates: {
            on: { label: 'Watching', variant: 'primary' },
            off: { label: 'Watch' },
          },
        },
        // Manual control: a flow sets the next state
        {
          id: 'deploy',
          label: 'Deploy',
          state: 'idle',
          states: {
            idle: { label: 'Deploy' },
            deploying: { label: 'Deploying…', disabled: true },
            deployed: { label: 'Deployed' },
          },
        },
      ],
      keepInteractive: true,
    },
  }],
});
```

The block answers with a `ButtonGroupResponse` — `{ buttonId, state }`. Responses reach flows as `interactive.message.response` with `{ messageId, threadId, response }`.

### Text editor extensions

`extensions.textEditor` declares named editor groups. Each group names its extensions,
popups and optional suggestion predicate without importing executable code:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "textEditor": {
      "suggestions": {
        "extensions": {
          "mention": "src/editor/mention.ts#Mention"
        },
        "popups": {
          "mentions": "src/editor/MentionPopup.vue"
        },
        "isSuggestionActive": "src/editor/suggestions.ts#isSuggestionActive"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Extension keys must match the exported Tiptap objects' names. Popup IDs belong to their group.
The predicate receives an editor state and returns `true` while suggestions should handle Escape.
Codegen imports each target and assembles keyed `TextEditorExtensions` bindings (`@apackdev/sdk/fe`);
there is no registration module or aggregate array export. Binding admission checks exact group/member
names and executable shapes without calling components or predicates. Conflicting active extension
names are refused with both owners. Catalog metadata remains available for disabled packs.

`textEditorExtensionsRegistry.getAll()` returns accepted `{ packId, groupId, binding }` rows in pack/group
registration order. Each row is immutable and its binding holds captured extension/popup membership.
The returned list is detached. `onChanged(listener)` observes committed frontend changes and returns
an unsubscribe function; reread the projection to distinguish relevant editor changes from unrelated
pack changes. Discovery and unavailable groups remain in the catalog, separate from these bindings.

Open editors follow accepted contribution changes. Popup/predicate-only updates preserve the editor
and undo history. Extension changes replace its executable editor after retiring the previous popups;
pending document data, selection and focus survive when the new schema represents them exactly.
Undo history resets on that replacement. An unavailable or incompatible schema suspends the editor
without saving altered content; restoring a compatible pack restores the pending document. Switching
to another document explicitly discards that suspended editor state.

The app's native frontend owns the `reference` node used by saved `apack-ref://` links. It is present
without default-setup or a reference provider. Default-setup adds `referenceSuggestion`, its picker
popups and command behavior; disabling it removes those tools while preserving stored references.
Packs cannot claim the native node name. Reference type/category metadata and eligible navigation
bindings still determine presentation and click behavior independently of parsing.

### Defining a system

```typescript
// src/features/bookmarks/be/contract.ts — the contract, which apack.json names at features.<id>.system.contract
import type { IncomingBookmarksEvents, OutgoingBookmarksEvents } from './types.ts';

export type Contract = {
  incoming: IncomingBookmarksEvents;
  outgoing: OutgoingBookmarksEvents;
};
```

```typescript
// src/features/bookmarks/be/types.ts — the events (SEND_STATE is the app's; no system declares it)
export type IncomingBookmarksEvents =
  | { type: 'CREATE_BOOKMARK'; url: string; title: string };

export type OutgoingBookmarksEvents =
  | { type: 'BOOKMARKS_CONNECTED'; data: BookmarkDTO[] }
  | { type: 'BOOKMARK_CREATED'; bookmark: BookmarkDTO };
```

```typescript
// src/features/bookmarks/be/system.ts
import { setup } from 'xstate';
import { defineSystem } from '@apackdev/sdk/framework';
// broadcastToSurface is typed with the events each of this pack's surfaces receives
import { broadcastToSurface } from '#generated/events.ts';
import type { Contract } from './contract.ts';

// The spec types the machine from the same contract. Its identity is its feature's, from apack.json
export const bookmarksSpec = defineSystem<Contract>();

// Build the machine
export const bookmarksSystem = setup({
  types: bookmarksSpec.types,
  actions: {
    sendConnectedData: () => {
      broadcastToSurface('bookmarks', {
        type: 'BOOKMARKS_CONNECTED',
        data: [],
      });
    },
  },
}).createMachine({
  id: 'bookmarks',
  initial: 'idle',
  states: {
    idle: {
      on: {
        SEND_STATE: { actions: 'sendConnectedData' },
      },
    },
  },
});

// Default-export the entry — this is what the manifest loads. Nothing to annotate and nothing to
// `satisfies`: the events come from the contract, which apack.json names
const bookmarksEntry = {
  spec: bookmarksSpec,
  machine: bookmarksSystem,
};

export default bookmarksEntry;
```

`defineSystem<Contract>()` returns the spec. It takes no id: the system is its feature's, which the manifest names it under, and runs at the feature's ref, `<packId>/<featureId>`; code names it by the feature id (see below).

The build materializes each endpoint's event names into `eventContracts` in the snapshot. Systems record
`incoming` and `outgoing`; surface `receives` combines its own system's outgoing events with its declared
inbox. Shared SDK lifecycle events are implicit in both protocols. `apack pack info --json` exposes this
under `build.metadata.eventContracts` without importing pack modules or requiring authored sources.
An installed build missing a declared endpoint's contract is refused; an unbuilt source preview reports
that metadata as unavailable. The catalog retains contracts when an owner is disabled or its runtime
fails. Runtime availability remains separate from this declaration inventory.

The contract is a **declared type**, not a value, and it lives in a leaf module of its own. `apack.json` names it at `features.<id>.system.contract` (`"src/features/bookmarks/be/contract.ts#Contract"`), and the build reads the type from there without running or resolving the machine. Its fields:

| Field | What it is |
|---|---|
| `incoming` | what anything outside the system sends it: another system, a surface, a dependent pack. Required unless the system only broadcasts |
| `internal` | what the system's own children send it (a `fromCallback` child telling its parent). These reach the machine's event union and nothing a dependent pack can see, so they can't be sent from outside. Optional |
| `outgoing` | every event the system sends to surfaces, and the one place they're declared: the build types `broadcastToSurface` from it and lists what each surface receives. Required |
| `context` | the machine's context, `{}` when omitted |

The spec it returns:

| Member | What it is |
|---|---|
| `types` | `{ context; events }`, for `setup({ types })`: the events are `incoming \| internal \| SystemEvents`. `SystemEvents` is `SEND_STATE`, `CLIENT_CONNECTED`, `PACK_CHANGED { packId }`, `DATA_REPLACED` and `FEATURE_SETTINGS_UPDATED { settings, changes }`, so no contract lists them |
| `typeOf` | `safeEvents` over the same events, to narrow an event by type in actions |

### Communication patterns

Your code names features: your own by id (`'bookmarks'`), and every other as `<packId>/<featureId>` (`'default-setup/logs'`; the app is the pack `host`, so its surface is `'host/application'`). Every API that reaches a system or surface takes that name. A feature's system and surface both run under its ref, `<packId>/<featureId>`, the same spelling you write for another pack's features: a bare name is only short for your own pack's. Actions name systems and surfaces `<packId>/<featureId>`, their own pack's included (see [`services.emitter`](services-and-data.md#host-services)).

| To | Use |
|---|---|
| send to a surface from a system | `broadcastToSurface(name, event)` (`#generated/events`) — every window |
| send to a surface from the frontend | `sendToSurface(name, event)` (`#generated/events`) — this window |
| send to a system, from a surface or from another system | `sendToSystem(name, event)` (`#generated/events`), typed with the events that system declares |
| send to whichever system plays a role | `sendToSystem({ role }, event)`: `sendToSystem({ role: 'brain' }, { type: 'TRIGGER_BRAIN_EVENT', eventType })` fires a flow event |
| reach your surface's actor from its components | `useSurface<MyActor>()` (`@apackdev/sdk/fe`): the app renders a surface's canvas, panel, chat and settings as part of it. Name your machine's actor type — which surface a component belongs to is where it is rendered, so nothing at the call site can infer it, and an unnamed one would read a context field your machine dropped and still compile |
| read another surface's state | `useSurfaceState(name, selector)` from `#generated/fe` in a component's setup or an effect scope, and `readSurfaceState(name, selector)` once, outside one (a machine's action). The selector takes that surface's published state, not an XState snapshot. Your own pack's surfaces are always running, so the value is never `undefined`; a dependency's frontend may still be loading, so reading one of its surfaces gives `undefined` until it arrives, and fills in when it does. For a ref that arrives as data, `useUntypedSurfaceState`/`readUntypedSurfaceState` (`@apackdev/sdk/fe`) are the untyped escape hatch |
| declare what other surfaces may send yours | the `inbox` half of your surface's `Contract`, a declared type in a leaf module `apack.json` names at `features.<id>.surface.contract`: `export type Contract = { state: MyContext; inbox: SurfaceInbox<{ public: MyInbox }> }`. Your own feature's system needs no declaration — its outgoing events are already your surface's. The `public` half is what a pack that depends on yours may send, and what types `sendToSurface` and `openSurface`. The leaf imports no machine, so codegen can read the contract without resolving one |
| offer another of your features your surface's state or events | put them in your surface's `Contract` — `state` is read through the generated readers, `inbox` types the sends. A feature imports nothing of another feature's frontend (`check:specifiers`), so the contract is the whole of what crosses |
| open a surface, optionally handing it events | `openSurface(name, event?)` (`#generated/fe`), which takes only the names your pack can write: its own features' and its dependencies'. The events are that surface's inbox, the same `sendToSurface` takes — this hands them to its actor too |
| open a surface a piece of data names (a link's target) | `untypedOpenSurface(ref, event?)` (`@apackdev/sdk/fe`). It throws for a string that isn't a `<packId>/<featureId>`; otherwise the app opens the surface, waiting while the pack that provides it is still loading, and tells the user if no installed pack provides it |

```typescript
import { broadcastToSurface, sendToSystem } from '#generated/events.ts';

// System -> Surface (via bus)
broadcastToSurface('bookmarks', { type: 'BOOKMARK_CREATED', bookmark });

// System -> System (via bus), checked against what the tags system declares it receives
sendToSystem('tags', { type: 'SOME_EVENT' });
```

Frontend code (and backend code) sends events to systems with `sendToSystem` from `#generated/events`:

```typescript
import { sendToSystem } from '#generated/events.ts';

// Surface -> System: this pack's systems by feature id, a dependency's as <dependency>/<feature>
sendToSystem('bookmarks', { type: 'CREATE_BOOKMARK', url, title });
sendToSystem('default-setup/notes', { type: 'EMPTY_TRASH' });
```

`sendToSystem` accepts only systems of your pack and its dependencies, and only the events each one declares (its contract's `incoming`); a missing field is reported against the event its `type` names. Each send names one system and one event type: a `systemId` or `type` typed as a union is rejected. A bare name is your own feature (`'bookmarks'` is `my-pack/bookmarks`). A dependency's system is always named with the dependency's id, so a feature of yours may share its name: `'notes'` is your own, `'default-setup/notes'` default-setup's. The app checks target and event-name membership against the accepted endpoint contract. The generated system inbox contains the contract's `incoming`, explicit `system.events.incoming` additions, and shared SDK lifecycle events. Private machine handlers do not define a public inbox; backend event diagnostics do not validate payloads or authorize senders.

Backend code that needs the connection or every incoming event subscribes with `onConnected(callback)` and `onIncoming(callback)` from `@apackdev/sdk/events`; each returns an unsubscribe function. Log entries arrive through `onLog(callback)` from `@apackdev/sdk/logger`.

### Frontend state machine

```typescript
// src/features/bookmarks/fe/state.ts
import { setup, type ActorRefFrom } from 'xstate';

// The feature's name: the machine's id, and how the pack's code sends to and looks up this surface
export const id = 'bookmarks';
export type BookmarksState = ActorRefFrom<typeof bookmarksState>;

const bookmarksState = setup({
  types: {
    context: {} as { items: unknown[] },
    events: {} as
      | { type: 'BOOKMARKS_CONNECTED'; data: unknown[] }
      | { type: 'BOOKMARK_CREATED'; bookmark: unknown },
  },
}).createMachine({
  id,
  initial: 'idle',
  context: { items: [] },
  states: {
    idle: {},
  },
});

export default bookmarksState;
```

## Settings

Each feature declares a JSON Schema and explicit defaults in `apack.json`; forms remain custom:

```json
{
  "features": {
    "bookmarks": {
      "description": "Save and organize bookmarks.",
      "settings": {
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "syncInterval": {
              "type": "integer",
              "minimum": 1
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "syncInterval"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        },
        "defaults": {
          "syncInterval": 300
        }
      },
      "surface": {
        "label": "Bookmarks",
        "icon": "Bookmark",
        "state": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/state.ts",
        "canvas": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/canvas/list.vue",
        "defaultVisible": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- `surface.default: true` claims the initial default. At most one feature per pack may claim it. Across packs, enabled native claims precede installed claims, then fully qualified feature refs sort in English lexical order. Selection uses declarations, so a faster frontend import cannot take another pack's default. A saved destination, popout target or explicit navigation takes precedence; the host remains usable while the selected frontend loads.
- `surface.defaultVisible` controls whether the sidebar tab shows by default (it shows when omitted). What the user shows or hides is the app's own state, kept by the host (`host/application`), and wins over it.
- The host stores overrides under `features.<packId>/<featureId>`; the declaration does not repeat that ID.
- Settings use JSON Schema draft-07 and required explicit `defaults`. Invalid declarations fail the build and registration; invalid changed values are refused at the shared store. Validation does not coerce, populate defaults or transform values. See [Feature settings](manifest.md#feature-settings).
- The settings are defaults: when your pack is enabled they join the app's defaults, and what the user changes is stored over them. Disabling the pack leaves its discovered definitions and defaults available; a new version's defaults apply to every key the user didn't change.
- Codegen derives the read and write types from the schema. Backend code uses `featureSettings.forFeature('bookmarks')` from `#generated/settings.ts`; components use `useSettings('bookmarks')` from `#generated/settings-fe.ts`. Own features use bare IDs; dependencies use `<packId>/<featureId>`. Only features declaring a settings schema are accepted. The frontend module loads no schemas or backend services.

```typescript
import { featureSettings } from '#generated/settings.ts';
const bookmarks = featureSettings.forFeature('bookmarks');
const interval = bookmarks.get().syncInterval; // number
bookmarks.set({ path: ['syncInterval'], value: 600 });
bookmarks.reset(); // removes overrides; applies the current defaults
const stop = bookmarks.onChange(value => console.log(value.syncInterval));
// Call stop() when this backend consumer is disposed.
```

Pack-wide groups are declared under `extensions.settings`, using the same `schema`, `defaults`, and
optional `form` contract. Backend code uses `packSettings.forGroup('assistant')` from
`#generated/settings`; frontend code uses `usePackSettings('assistant')` from `#generated/settings-fe`.
Their values live at `packs[packId][groupId]`, so two packs can declare `assistant` independently.
Bare selectors address the importing pack; qualified selectors name a declared dependency or the
importing pack explicitly. `access: "owner"` makes dependency handles read-only in generated types;
shared read/write/reset is the default. App preferences remain host-owned at `app`.

Setting a value equal to its current default records a choice. Reset removes that choice and follows
future defaults. Settings consumers and subscriptions work independently of opening the Settings view.

The host's advanced JSON editor shows the complete effective document (`app`, `packs`, `features`).
Installed declarations supply completion, descriptions and validation, including disabled packs.
Saving records the entire document as explicit choices; omitted required fields are errors rather than
requests to inherit defaults. Use the settings reset controls to resume following defaults. Unavailable
owners remain visible and may be retained or removed, but their values cannot be edited until their
declarations are available again. Backup files contain sparse overrides and use the import contract.

In a component, `useSettings('bookmarks')` returns a reactive `value` (undefined until loaded), typed `set`, and `reset`. It must run in a component or effect scope; disposal stops its subscription. `useAppSettings()` from `#generated/settings-fe.ts` returns the same handle for host app preferences, using the pack's accepted caller access. Generated handles refuse writes after unload. Ambient SDK reads do not grant a write connection.

Custom forms type their props with `SettingsOf<'bookmarks'>` and their `update-setting` payload with `SettingUpdate<'bookmarks'>`, both from `#generated/settings-fe.ts`. Paths address object fields, including record keys, up to six levels; replace arrays as a whole. Each path is paired with its field's value type. Runtime validation remains authoritative. Forms emit updates to their host owner, which supplies validation results and reset behavior. `useSettingsSave(writer)` from `@apackdev/sdk/fe` tracks requests through an explicitly supplied writer; one form cannot mark another saved or implicitly borrow window administration.

Custom forms can use `useSettingsDraft` from `@apackdev/sdk/fe` for an editable copy of a JSON settings field:

```typescript
import { useSettingsDraft } from '@apackdev/sdk/fe';
const tags = useSettingsDraft(() => props.settings.tags);
// Save explicitly: emit('update-setting', { path: ['tags'], value: tags.value });
```

Draft edits never mutate the settings snapshot. A persisted change to the selected field replaces its draft,
including after a reset; unrelated or equivalent snapshots preserve pending edits, regardless of object key order.
Array order is significant. Values must be JSON-compatible (or `undefined`). The composable requires a component
or effect scope; disposal stops its watcher. It does not save automatically.

The host's raw `services.settings` and frontend settings port support migrations, app-wide sections, and transport. Ordinary feature code uses the generated accessors.
- When a feature's settings change, however they changed (a setting, the settings replaced or reset, a pack's defaults coming or going), its system gets `FEATURE_SETTINGS_UPDATED { settings, changes }` (`changes`: what changed in each list, by its key) and its surface `FEATURE_SETTINGS_UPDATED { settings }`. Every system and surface receives it, like `SEND_STATE`: nothing declares it, and a feature without a system or a surface just doesn't get that half.

### Pack settings declarations

Pack groups are keyed by a local ID: a lowercase letter followed by letters or digits. The catalog
keeps each group's owner, so two packs can both declare `assistant` independently.

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "settings": {
      "assistant": {
        "title": "Assistant",
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": { "name": { "type": "string" } },
          "required": ["name"],
          "additionalProperties": false
        },
        "defaults": { "name": "" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Require explicit `schema` and `defaults`. Schemas use draft-07 and document-local references; validation
does not coerce values, populate schema default annotations or remove properties. Optional metadata is
`title`, `description`, `form` (a custom component's source path), and `access` (`shared` or `owner`).

Current implementation: declarations are validated and readable as catalog data without activation.
Pack group value handles, access enforcement and custom-form binding are still being integrated.
The feature settings interface below remains the current feature API until its migration is complete.

## Features

The `features` map is the primary way to add functionality. Each entry is keyed by its feature id and bundles a backend system, frontend surface, settings, and services.

```json
{
  "features": {
    "bookmarks": {
      "description": "Save and organize bookmarks.",
      "designation": "bookmarks",
      "settings": {
        "schema": { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false },
        "defaults": {}
      },
      "system": {
        "entry": "src/features/bookmarks/be/system.ts"
      },
      "surface": {
        "label": "Bookmarks",
        "icon": "Bookmark",
        "state": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/state.ts",
        "canvas": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/canvas/list.vue"
      },
      "services": {
        "bookmarks": "src/features/bookmarks/be/services/bookmarks.ts#bookmarksService"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Feature settings

A feature declares effective-value validation and explicit defaults as JSON data. Codegen derives its TypeScript value type from the schema:

```json
{
  "features": {
    "bookmarks": {
      "description": "Save and organize bookmarks.",
      "entities": { "Bookmark": { "shape": "src/features/bookmarks/be/types.ts#BookmarkAttributes" } },
      "settings": {
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "syncInterval": {
              "type": "integer",
              "minimum": 1
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "syncInterval"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        },
        "defaults": {
          "syncInterval": 300
        }
      },
      "surface": {
        "label": "Bookmarks",
        "icon": "Bookmark",
        "state": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/state.ts",
        "canvas": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/canvas/list.vue",
        "defaultVisible": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The declaration owns only this feature's settings; it does not repeat the feature ID. The host stores user overrides under `features.<packId>/<featureId>` and validates changed values for every write, including custom forms, actions and the JSON editor. Forms remain custom Vue components; no form is generated from the schema. Use JSON Schema draft-07 for validation; values are never coerced, transformed or populated from schema annotations. References stay within the value schema.

Defaults must pass the schema before the pack builds or registers. Navigation visibility is separate: `surface.defaultVisible: false` needs no settings declaration. Settings may also declare presentation metadata and a custom `form` path. Writes must satisfy the current schema; unchanged invalid values are not exempt. A disabled pack retains its user overrides, but its schema must be loaded before those values can be edited.

Installation, replacement, shipped-pack updates and live reload validate stored overrides against the
candidate settings declarations before replacing accepted state. Incompatible changes are refused with
the settings owner, field and reason; the incumbent remains accepted. This also checks overrides whose
pack was previously unavailable when its declaration becomes available again.

If preferences are already invalid at startup, the host opens a recovery screen before loading
external packs. Recovery can explicitly reset selected affected groups in one transaction, preserving
unselected groups, or reset all preferences. Every preference in a selected group returns to its default. Malformed document structure requires the complete reset.
Nothing is converted or silently discarded. After repair, restart apack to initialize its packs;
reconnecting a window preserves that restart requirement. App data and API keys are not reset.

Declare a custom form at `features.<id>.settings.form`, for example
`"form": "src/features/bookmarks/fe/settings.vue"`. The module default-exports a Vue
component that receives `settings` and emits `update-setting` with `{ path, value, condition? }`.
The host supplies the target address. Forms are independent of surfaces: a feature can provide a
form without a surface or system.

Pack-wide preferences use the same definition under `extensions.settings.<groupId>`, including
`schema`, `defaults`, optional `title` and `description`, and optional `form`. Their values live at
`packs[packId][groupId]`. Use a group for preferences shared across features; app preferences belong
to the host. Pack and feature declarations remain visible in Settings when their executable form
is unavailable; the JSON editor remains available. There is no pack-owned assembly of app settings.

## Steps

Flow step types are declared under `extensions.steps`, keyed by type.

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "steps": {
      "my-step": {
        "node": {
          "label": "My Step",
          "defaultLabel": "Run my step"
        },
        "build": {
          "compile": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#compile",
          "validate": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#validate",
          "getLabel": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/build.ts#getLabel"
        },
        "fe": {
          "nodeConfig": {
            "icon": "Box",
            "isImplemented": true,
            "category": "logic",
            "connectionRules": {
              "inputs": -1,
              "outputs": -1
            }
          },
          "colorKey": "indigo",
          "components": {
            "form": {
              "entry": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/form.vue",
              "load": "lazy"
            }
          }
        },
        "runtime": {
          "handler": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/runtime.ts#handler",
          "isAsync": true
        },
        "dsl": {
          "primaryField": "action"
        },
        "types": "src/extensions/steps/my-step/types.ts"
      },
      "my-trigger": {
        "kind": "trigger",
        "node": {
          "label": "My Trigger",
          "defaults": {
            "source": "change me"
          }
        },
        "trigger": {
          "trackField": "myTrigger",
          "compile": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/build.ts#compile",
          "decompile": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/build.ts#decompile",
          "register": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/runtime.ts#register"
        },
        "fe": {
          "nodeConfig": {
            "icon": "Radio",
            "isImplemented": true,
            "category": "trigger",
            "connectionRules": {
              "inputs": 0,
              "outputs": -1
            }
          },
          "colorKey": "blue",
          "components": {
            "form": {
              "entry": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/form.vue",
              "load": "lazy"
            }
          }
        },
        "types": "src/extensions/steps/my-trigger/types.ts"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Codegen sends each facet where it is used, so no barrel keeps them in step with each other: `build` and
`runtime` go to the pack's backend registration, `fe` to its frontend one, and `build` (or `trigger`) alone
to `src/__generated__/steps-build.ts`, which `apack pack build` bundles to `dist/build/steps.build.mjs` — the
module a *dependent* pack's build loads to validate its flows with your step code.

Step `types` and `dsl.helpers` name pack-root-relative modules explicitly. No files are inferred from
facet directories; build, layout, form and type modules may live in different directories.

## Migrations

A migration moves data the pack has already written into the shape its new code expects. Each is declared
under the **version line** its target is on, keyed by the version it targets:

```json
{
  "migrations": {
    "pack": { "1.2.0": { "description": "Give every memo a colour", "up": "src/migrations/1.2.0.ts#up" } },
    "app": { "0.4.0": { "description": "Update app-owned data", "up": "src/migrations/0.4.0.ts#up" } }
  }
}
```

| Line | The target is | Runs when |
|---|---|---|
| `pack` | a version of **this pack**, compared against `version` in this manifest | the pack updates past it |
| `app` | a release of **apack** | the app updates past it |

Most migrations belong on `pack`: the pack's data moves when the pack does. Use `app` for data whose shape
follows apack's rather than yours. A target is always three numbers — the app reads a prerelease as its
release, so `0.4.0-beta.2` would match nothing.

`apack pack add migration 1.2.0` writes the entry and the file; `--app` puts it on the app's line.

The declaration owns the version line, target and description. The module exports behavior only:

```ts
// src/migrations/1.2.0.ts
export function up(): void {
  // Apply the change synchronously, checking first whether it is needed.
}
```

Keep migrations idempotent: development boots, prereleases and resets may repeat them. Descriptions are
available in the installed snapshot even when the pack is disabled or cannot activate.

A pack may declare both lines. They run at different moments, so a single change split across them gets an
order you probably did not intend — keep one change on one line.

## Editor and app extensions

Declare these contributions directly under `extensions`, alongside artifacts, help and other kinds:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "textEditor": {
      "suggestions": {
        "extensions": { "mention": "src/editor/mention.ts#Mention" },
        "popups": { "mentions": "src/editor/MentionPopup.vue" },
        "isSuggestionActive": "src/editor/suggestions.ts#isSuggestionActive"
      }
    },
    "appExtensions": { "welcome": "src/extensions/app/Welcome.vue" }
  }
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `textEditor` | `Record<string, TextEditorExtensionsDeclaration>` | Named groups of editor extensions, popups and optional suggestion predicates |
| `appExtensions` | `Record<string, string>` | Named app extensions: slot name (an identifier) → Vue component path |

Within each Tiptap group, `extensions` maps the actual Tiptap name to its exported extension object
(`path#export`); `popups` maps local popup IDs to Vue modules; `isSuggestionActive` names a function
export. All paths are pack-root-relative. Discovery reads these declarations without importing their
modules. Build checks exports and runtime admission checks exact member identities and executable
shapes. Conflicting active extension names are refused with both owners.
See [Text editor extensions](extensions.md#text-editor-extensions) for the binding contract.

Carrying your own copy of `@apackdev/ui` is `build.bundleUi`, not one of these: it changes what the build
produces rather than giving the app anything.

The frontend entry itself isn't declared here: `apack pack build` bundles `src/pack-entry-fe.ts` (or `.js`) if present, else the generated `src/__generated__/pack-entry-fe.ts`, into the pack's `runtime/fe.js`, with any extracted styles as `runtime/fe.css`. The app loads whichever of those two files the installed pack has.

## DSL definitions

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "dsl": {
      "action": {
        "entry": "src/defs/action.ts",
        "targets": ["monaco"],
        "prefix": "action:",
        "inline": ["ai"],
        "globals": { "services": "typeof _dsl.services" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `entry` | `string` | Module whose types are bundled into the definitions |
| `targets` | `["monaco"]` | Editors that get the definitions |
| `prefix` | `string` | Editor models whose path starts with it get these definitions (e.g. `action:`). Omission or an empty value disables path inference; an editor can select the DSL explicitly |
| `inline` | `string[]` | Packages whose declarations are bundled into the definitions besides your own modules and `@apackdev/*`. The editor loads no `node_modules`, so a type it needs from another package belongs here; everything else stays an import |
| `globals` | `Record<string, string>` | Globals in scope and their types. The catalog carries these declarations with the compiled definition text; Monaco follows updates without activating a frontend |

Path inference uses the unique longest matching nonempty prefix; equal-length matches from different DSLs are ambiguous. DSL files default to TypeScript unless the editor explicitly selects another language.

For each entry with a `monaco` target, `apack pack build` writes `dist/defs/monaco/<name>-defs.d.ts`: the entry's types bundled into one declaration file, wrapped as `declare module "@app/defs/<name>"`.

